---
import GuideLayout from "@/layouts/GuideLayout.astro";
import { GUIDES_BY_SLUG } from "@/data/guides";
const g = GUIDES_BY_SLUG["time-clock-rounding-rules"]!;
---

<GuideLayout title={g.title} metaTitle={g.metaTitle} description={g.description} slug={g.slug} updated={g.updated}>
  <p>
    Many employers do not pay from the exact minute you clock in. They
    <strong>round</strong> punch times to a set increment — usually the nearest
    5, 6, or 15 minutes — before calculating pay. US federal rules allow this,
    but only within limits. This guide explains the well-known "7-minute rule",
    when rounding is lawful, and how to check whether it is costing you.
  </p>

  <h2>Why employers round</h2>
  <p>
    Rounding dates back to mechanical punch clocks and is still used to keep
    payroll tidy. The FLSA regulation (29 CFR 785.48) permits recording time
    "to the nearest 5 minutes, or to the nearest one-tenth or quarter of an
    hour", <em>provided</em> the practice does not, over time, fail to
    compensate employees for all the time they actually work.
  </p>

  <h2>The 7-minute rule (15-minute rounding)</h2>
  <p>
    With quarter-hour rounding, each punch is moved to the nearest :00, :15,
    :30, or :45. The midpoint is 7&frac12; minutes:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Clock in at <strong>8:07</strong> &rarr; rounds back to 8:00 (you gain up to 7 minutes).</li>
    <li>Clock in at <strong>8:08</strong> &rarr; rounds forward to 8:15 (you lose up to 7 minutes).</li>
  </ul>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Actual punch</th><th>Minutes past the quarter</th><th>Rounds to</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>9:00–9:07</td><td>0–7</td><td>9:00</td></tr>
      <tr><td>9:08–9:14</td><td>8–14</td><td>9:15</td></tr>
      <tr><td>9:15–9:22</td><td>0–7</td><td>9:15</td></tr>
      <tr><td>9:23–9:29</td><td>8–14</td><td>9:30</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>When rounding is legal — and when it is not</h2>
  <p>
    Rounding is acceptable only if it is <strong>neutral</strong>: over a period
    of time it should balance out, sometimes favouring the employee and
    sometimes the employer. It is unlawful when it is systematically
    one-sided, for example:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Rounding clock-ins <em>up</em> and clock-outs <em>down</em> (always against the worker).</li>
    <li>Only rounding in the employer's favour ("grace periods" that never add time).</li>
    <li>Editing or auto-deducting time for work that was actually performed, such as a missed break the employee worked through.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Courts have increasingly scrutinised rounding, and some states —
    <strong>California</strong> in particular — have effectively rejected it for
    hourly employees where exact time records exist. Where state law is stricter
    or does not allow rounding, the state rule wins.
  </p>

  <h2>Rounding is not the same as decimal conversion</h2>
  <p>
    Turning 8 hours 45 minutes into 8.75 is exact arithmetic, not rounding — see
    <a href="/guides/convert-minutes-to-decimal-hours/">how to convert minutes
    to decimal hours</a>. Rounding changes the <em>times</em> before any
    conversion happens.
  </p>

  <h2>Work that happens before or after a punch</h2>
  <p>
    Rounding cannot be used to avoid paying for real work. If you are required
    to be at your station, boot up systems, or put on protective gear before
    clocking in, that time is generally compensable ("donning and doffing" and
    preliminary/postliminary activity rules). A rounding policy does not
    override that.
  </p>

  <h2>How to check your own total</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Write down your actual in and out times for the week from your own notes.</li>
    <li>Add up the real minutes worked and convert once to decimal hours.</li>
    <li>Compare with the hours on your pay stub.</li>
    <li>A small difference that sometimes favours you is normal. A difference that is always against you, week after week, is a red flag.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Raise a persistent shortfall with payroll in writing. Unpaid-time claims go
    to the Department of Labor's Wage and Hour Division or your state labour
    agency, and back pay can usually be recovered for two years.
  </p>

  <h2>Add up your real hours</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/calculators/time-clock-calculator/">Time Clock Calculator</a>
    turns a list of in/out punches into a decimal total with no rounding, so you
    can compare it against your paid hours. The
    <a href="/calculators/time-card-calculator/">Time Card Calculator</a> does
    the same across a full week with breaks and overtime.
  </p>

  <h2>Sources</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-29/subtitle-B/chapter-V/subchapter-B/part-785/subject-group-ECFR0f4a132da22cf82/section-785.48" rel="noopener">29 CFR 785.48 — Use of time clocks (rounding practices)</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/fact-sheets/22-flsa-hours-worked" rel="noopener">US Department of Labor — Fact Sheet #22: Hours Worked Under the FLSA</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/fact-sheets/21-flsa-recordkeeping" rel="noopener">US Department of Labor — Fact Sheet #21: Recordkeeping</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://www.dir.ca.gov/dlse/faq_hoursworked.htm" rel="noopener">California DIR — Hours Worked FAQ</a></li>
  </ul>
</GuideLayout>
